Now I have the complete picture. Here's how errors propagate through the composition path:

## Error propagation path

**1. Where an error originates** — inside a middleware/handler called from `dispatch(i)` in `src/compose.ts`:
```ts
res = await handler(context, () => dispatch(i + 1))
```
Any synchronous throw or rejected promise from `handler` (or from any downstream middleware it calls via `next()`, since they're all nested in the same `dispatch` recursion / call stack) is caught right there by the surrounding `try/catch` in `dispatch`.

**2. First catch point — `dispatch()` in `compose.ts`:**
```ts
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof Error && onError) {
    context.error = err
    res = await onError(err, context)
    isError = true
  } else {
    throw err
  }
}
```
- Only `Error` instances (or subclasses, e.g. `HTTPException` from `src/http-exception.ts`) are handled — non-`Error` throws (e.g. `throw "string"`) skip the `onError` branch and propagate up unchanged.
- If handled: the error is stashed on `context.error` (readable by later middleware per the doc comment at `context.ts:319`), and `onError` (Hono's per-app error handler) is invoked to produce a `Response`. The `isError` flag forces `context.res = res` even if `context.finalized` was already true, since normally responses are only applied `if (context.finalized === false)`.
- If `onError` itself throws, or no `onError` was supplied, the error is rethrown out of `dispatch(i)`, which unwinds through every enclosing `dispatch` call (i.e., through every middleware "onion layer" above it) — this is why upstream middleware's own `try/catch` around `await next()` can intercept errors from downstream middleware.
- **`onError` here is `this.errorHandler` from `Hono`, passed in at compose-call time** — so this per-request `onError` is the *same* handler for every layer; only the outermost `compose()` call actually receives it (`compose(matchResult[0], this.errorHandler, this.#notFoundHandler)` in `hono-base.ts`), meaning any middleware that doesn't catch the error itself lets it bubble all the way to this single handler.

**3. Second catch point — `#dispatch()` in `src/hono-base.ts`:**
```ts
const composed = compose(matchResult[0], this.errorHandler, this.#notFoundHandler)
return (async () => {
  try {
    const context = await composed(c)
    ...
  } catch (err) {
    return this.#handleError(err, c)
  }
})()
```
This is the outer safety net for anything that escaped `compose`'s internal handling (non-`Error` throws, or errors thrown by `errorHandler` itself, or the "Context is not finalized" error thrown when a handler neither returns a `Response` nor calls `next()`).

- `#handleError`:
```ts
#handleError(err: unknown, c: Context<E>): Response | Promise<Response> {
  if (err instanceof Error) {
    return this.errorHandler(err, c)
  }
  throw err
}
```
Only re-invokes `this.errorHandler` for genuine `Error` instances; anything else is rethrown (ultimately surfacing as an unhandled rejection/throw out of `fetch()`).

- The single-handler fast path (when `matchResult[0].length === 1`, i.e. no middleware, `compose` is skipped entirely) has its own inline `try/catch` around the direct handler call, also routing to `#handleError`.

**4. Default error handler** (`errorHandler` const in `hono-base.ts`, used unless overridden via `app.onError()`):
```ts
const errorHandler: ErrorHandler = (err, c) => {
  if ('getResponse' in err) {
    const res = err.getResponse()
    return c.newResponse(res.body, res)
  }
  console.error(err)
  return c.text('Internal Server Error', 500)
}
```
- Detects `HTTPException` (or any error duck-typed with `getResponse()`, defined in `src/http-exception.ts`) and converts it to a proper `Response` via `HTTPException.getResponse()`, which builds a `Response` from `this.res` (if supplied) or `this.message`/`this.status`.
- Any other `Error` is logged via `console.error` and converted to a generic 500 `Response`.
- A custom handler registered with `app.onError((err, c) => ...)` (sets `this.errorHandler` in `hono-base.ts`) replaces this entirely and is what actually gets passed into `compose()`.

**Summary of the propagation chain:**
```
handler/middleware throws
  → caught in compose.ts dispatch() [per-layer try/catch]
      → if Error & onError given: onError(err, ctx) runs, res written to ctx.res, layer returns normally (error "swallowed" at this layer)
      → else: rethrown, unwinds through enclosing dispatch() frames (i.e. through outer middleware's own try/catch around next(), if any)
  → if uncaught anywhere in compose: propagates out of composed(c) in hono-base.ts #dispatch()
      → caught by outer try/catch → #handleError(err, c) → this.errorHandler(err, c) (custom via onError() or default) → Response
  → non-Error throws bypass all error-handler logic and are rethrown all the way out of fetch()
```

### What I did not verify
- I didn't trace how a custom `onError` set via `app.onError()` interacts with sub-app error handlers combined through `.route()` (the `compose([], app.errorHandler)` wrapping seen earlier) in a multi-level nested-app scenario beyond reading the single code block already shown.
- I didn't check test files (`compose.test.ts`, `hono.test.ts`, `http-exception.test.ts`) for documented edge cases (e.g., behavior when `onError` itself throws, or interaction with `context.finalized` timing).
- I didn't check adapter-specific code (`src/adapter/*`) for any platform-specific error handling layered on top of `fetch()`.
